    [34mreturn[39m { [36msuccess[39m: [34mtrue[39m, [36muser[39m: username };  [32m// stub, would POST to API_ENDPOINT[39m
  }

  One caller, [38;5;153mapp.js:23[39m, inside the form submit handler. The form ([38;5;153mindex.html:9-11[39m) collects only username and password.

  [1mThe fork:[0m there is no userId in scope at the call site. Nothing in this app knows a userId before login happens —
  identity is what login establishes. So "add a userId parameter" can mean three different things, and they differ in
  what the interface promises:

  - [1mReturn the userId instead of taking one[0m — server (here, the stub) resolves identity from the credentials and hands
    it back; the handler logs it. Buys correct tracking with no spoofable input. Costs: not literally a new parameter,
    so if you have an upstream caller that already holds a userId, this is the wrong shape. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mAdd a client-supplied [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m. Buys exactly what you asked for. Costs:
    the caller has nothing to pass, so I'd also add a form field or a source for it, and a client-asserted identity at
    the auth boundary is trustable only as a hint, never for authorization. Hard to walk back once callers depend on it.
  - [1mAdd a tracking/correlation ID parameter[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, trackingId)[39m with a client-generated ID, for
    correlating the attempt in logs. Buys attempt-level tracing that works before identity is known. Costs: it tracks
    the attempt, not the person.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the first. "Track who logged in" is answered by the identity login resolves, and taking that
  identity as an input at the auth boundary is the one version with a security downside.
